---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import ExplanationList from "../../../components/story/ExplanationList.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, n, chronology, explanations } from "../../../data/air-traffic-control-2026-11-13-faa-nationwide-ground-stop.mjs";
const story = "air-traffic-control--2026-11-13-faa-nationwide-ground-stop";
const short = (id) => claim(story, id).short;
const timeline = chronology.map((e) => ({ date: e.date, title: short(e.c), current: e.current, cites: [{ c: e.c }] }));
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/air-traffic-control/">Air traffic control</a><span>November 13, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Air traffic control &middot; November 13, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="national-stop-order" /><Cite c="stop-lifted" /><Cite c="duffy-switches-overloaded" /><Cite c="mullin-patches-appears" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-broke">What broke</a></li>
          <li><a href="#explanations">Every explanation, tested</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">What each side says</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">At 3:53 p.m. Eastern on Friday, November 13, the Federal Aviation Administration ordered every airport in the country to hold all departing flights until further notice.<Cite c="national-stop-order" /> Radar and radio links at the centers that guide planes between airports, and at the facilities that handle them near big airports, had become unstable, the transportation secretary later said, and the FAA called it a temporary telecommunications outage.<Cite c="duffy-onset-336" /><Cite c="faa-telecom-outage" /> It was the second nationwide ground stop since the September 11 attacks, two people at the FAA told MS NOW; the first, in January 2023, came after contractors unintentionally deleted files in the system that sends pilots safety notices.<Cite c="second-since-911" /><Cite c="notam-2023" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">{n.airborne} flights bound for U.S. airports were in the air when the order came, by Flightradar24's count, and they were told to expect holding wherever they landed.<Cite c="airborne-4042" /><Cite c="airborne-holding" /> At least {n.diverted} had been diverted by early evening, most of them back to where they took off, by Cirium's count, and by 7:15 p.m. FlightAware counted {n.cancelled} cancellations for the day.<Cite c="diversions-600" /><Cite c="flightaware-715" /> Europe's air traffic agency held U.S.-bound departures at the FAA's request, and Mexico grounded its flights to the United States.<Cite c="abroad-held" /> Alaska Airlines kept flying within Alaska and between the Hawaiian islands.<Cite c="alaska-hawaii-flew" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The FAA lifted the stop at 8:16 p.m., {n.hours} hours and {n.minutes} minutes after ordering it.<Cite c="stop-lifted" /> JetBlue expects cancellations to continue into Saturday.<Cite c="jetblue-into-saturday" /> Asked about an attack, Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy deferred to Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin and his cybersecurity team, and said he did not want to rule one out. Mullin says investigators have seen no evidence of one so far and are still looking.<Cite c="duffy-not-rule-out-nefarious" /><Cite c="mullin-zero-evidence" /> The FAA has not said what made its equipment fail on Friday.<Cite c="duffy-still-working" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="what-broke">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What broke</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">At a briefing that night, Duffy put the failure in the FAA's own telecommunications equipment.<Cite c="briefing-that-night" /> Its old analog switches and routers were overloaded with data and began sending inconsistent data, he said, so the FAA shut the system down.<Cite c="duffy-switches-overloaded" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Sean Duffy, Transportation Secretary" when="November 13, 2026" where="White House briefing" label="The White House">
          <p>"Our switches uh our routers actually got overloaded with data and they went down and uh I shouldn't say they went down they started to give us inconsistency in the data uh from those uh from those routers, and because of that, we shut the system down."<Cite c="duffy-switches-overloaded" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The newer digital switches held, Duffy said, and so did the centers and approach facilities already moved onto them.<Cite c="duffy-new-held" /> The FAA brought the networks back, he said, by deploying fixes that reduced the load on its switches.<Cite c="duffy-fix-load" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The trouble began 43 minutes before the national order. At 3:10 p.m. the FAA ground-stopped O'Hare for communications issues, and by Duffy's account radar and communications at its centers and approach facilities across the country became unstable at 3:36.<Cite c="sequence-chicago" /><Cite c="duffy-onset-336" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Friday afternoon, from the FAA's command center</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} heading="What happened" />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The contractor L3Harris runs the FAA's telecommunications network and says it uses several local phone carriers. It says the network carries all of the system's critical traffic, voice and radar included, and connects every tower, radar site and center.<Cite c="fti-multi-carrier" /><Cite c="fti-carries-all" /> The old part of it, Duffy said, "went down everywhere."<Cite c="duffy-everywhere" /> FAA Administrator Bryan Bedford told Congress in September that the network is a 1980s design, and L3Harris said in May it was halfway through rebuilding it while it ran.<Cite c="bedford-1980s-network" /><Cite c="fti-half-rebuilt" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In September a Philadelphia facility lost its lines after L3Harris had the FAA switch to backup lines and a contractor then accidentally cut the backup. Bedford said then that "we just don’t have the redundancy that we would get if we had a modern architecture."<Cite c="sept-2026-backup-cut" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="explanations">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Every explanation, tested</h2>
        <ExplanationList entries={explanations} />
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What each side says</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The administration.</strong> Duffy says the old system is the problem and Congress should pay to replace it: he wants ${n.duffyAsk} billion and says more outages will come without it.<Cite c="duffy-wants-50b" /><Cite c="duffy-switches-overloaded" /> Hours before the stop, the White House had asked Congress for ${n.requested} billion more for aviation.<Cite c="funding-30b" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>Senate Commerce Republicans.</strong> The committee's majority said the outage appears related to the en-route centers, which guide planes between airports, and shows the need to put modernizing the centers ahead of other projects, citing the $1.9 billion Chairman Ted Cruz secured to replace and consolidate them.<Cite c="senate-commerce-claim" /><Cite c="senate-commerce-19b" /> Cruz has also backed the money for new telecommunications.<Cite c="cruz-telecom-money" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>Democrats.</strong> Sen. Tammy Duckworth wants a full investigation, and Sen. Maria Cantwell says the Commerce Committee will review the main and backup radio frequencies controllers use.<Cite c="investigations" /> Rep. Delia Ramirez asked whether the administration's cuts and changes to the FAA were responsible.<Cite c="ramirez-cuts-question" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>"Old hardware" says what broke, not why it broke on Friday</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The administration's explanation is that old equipment failed and Congress should fund a new system: Duffy wants ${n.duffyAsk} billion and warns of more outages without it.<Cite c="duffy-wants-50b" /><Cite c="duffy-switches-overloaded" /> Asked what was different on Friday, Duffy said the FAA is still working on the details of what went wrong.<Cite c="duffy-still-working" /> The one trigger anyone has named on the record came from Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin: what it appears to be, he said, is that new equipment and patches the FAA was putting in were overwhelming the older system. Asked whether the patching caused it, Duffy described the old system being overloaded and did not say what overloaded it.<Cite c="mullin-patches-appears" /><Cite c="duffy-did-not-confirm-patching" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Multiple sources told CBS News an update to the network made it reboot and crash, and a federal official told MS NOW a software update caused the stop.<Cite c="cbs-update-crash" /><Cite c="msnow-software-update" /> Duffy has not confirmed that a change caused the failure.<Cite c="duffy-did-not-confirm-patching" /> He blames the old hardware and asks for $50 billion, while the one trigger named on the record is Mullin's: new equipment going in, which he said appears to have overwhelmed the old system. L3Harris said in May that it was halfway through rebuilding the network.<Cite c="duffy-switches-overloaded" /><Cite c="duffy-wants-50b" /><Cite c="mullin-patches-appears" /><Cite c="fti-rebuild-context" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">JetBlue expects more cancellations into Saturday.<Cite c="jetblue-into-saturday" /> Duffy says the FAA is still working out what went wrong. The FBI is looking at whether a software update was the cause, a person familiar told Bloomberg.<Cite c="duffy-still-working" /><Cite c="fbi-software-inquiry" /> Cantwell's committee plans its review.<Cite c="investigations" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
